WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.994
A validation workflow should make accepted and rejected behavior visible.

2
00:00:03.994 --> 00:00:11.184
Tests should include ordinary valid inputs, invalid inputs, boundary values, malformed structures, missing fields, duplicate fields, wrong units,

3
00:00:11.184 --> 00:00:15.178
large counts, edge indexes, unusual text, and resource-limit cases.

4
00:00:15.178 --> 00:00:17.175
Negative tests are especially important.

5
00:00:17.175 --> 00:00:20.770
They confirm that invalid input remains outside trusted state.

6
00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:28.758
When a validation defect is fixed, add a regression test that describes the intended contract, not just the old symptom.

7
00:00:28.758 --> 00:00:33.152
Over time, the test suite becomes executable documentation for the boundary.

8
00:00:33.152 --> 00:00:38.744
Defensive fuzzing can help at a high level when applied to parsers and validators.

9
00:00:38.744 --> 00:00:47.930
The goal is not spectacle; it is to explore unexpected combinations and confirm that the program fails cleanly, preserves invariants, and does not

10
00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:49.128
consume unreasonable resources.

11
00:00:49.128 --> 00:00:51.525
Code review should ask consistent questions.

12
00:00:51.525 --> 00:00:53.123
Where does input enter?

13
00:00:53.123 --> 00:00:57.117
What shape, type, range, encoding, and resource limits are expected?

14
00:00:57.117 --> 00:00:58.714
Is parse success checked?

15
00:00:58.714 --> 00:01:00.312
Are partial parses rejected?

16
00:01:00.312 --> 00:01:02.709
Are raw and trusted values separated?

17
00:01:02.709 --> 00:01:05.505
Are numeric conversions and derived sizes reviewed?

18
00:01:05.505 --> 00:01:08.700
Tooling helps, but it does not replace design.

19
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:14.292
Compiler warnings, sanitizers, static analysis, and CI checks can identify risky patterns and regressions.

20
00:01:14.292 --> 00:01:19.884
They are most valuable when the team has already made the validation contract explicit.

21
00:01:19.884 --> 00:01:21.082
Ownership also matters.

22
00:01:21.082 --> 00:01:28.671
Each input boundary should have a clear maintainer, documented expectations, and tests that run as part of normal development.

23
00:01:28.671 --> 00:01:34.662
Validation quality improves when it is treated as ordinary engineering work instead of emergency cleanup.

24
00:01:34.662 --> 00:01:42.251
During review, it helps to trace one accepted input and one rejected input from entry point to final decision.

25
00:01:42.251 --> 00:01:49.840
That exercise often reveals missing parse checks, repeated conversions, unclear logging, or trusted values that were never clearly created.
